<section class="section section-alt" id="history">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <span class="section-tag">The Car</span>
      <h2>A Herald underneath, a Michelotti body on top.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="essay reveal">
      <div>
        <span class="figure-tag">Origin, 1962</span>
        <p>
          Giovanni Michelotti drew the Spitfire around the Triumph Herald's frame,
          drivetrain, and suspension. It arrived in October 1962 as Triumph's answer to
          the small British sports car class, going head to head with the MG Midget and
          its rivals.
        </p>
        <p>
          What set it apart was the Herald-derived independent rear suspension and
          roll-up windows — comfort and handling usually reserved for cars costing a
          good deal more.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="pull">
          "Same silhouette, three series, one long-running argument about cornering
          stability."
        </p>
        <span class="figure-tag">Engines, across two decades</span>
        <p>
          The Spitfire began with a 1,147cc four borrowed from the Herald line. The Mk3
          grew it to 1,296cc with a redesigned head. After the MkIV, the block was
          stroked out to 1,493cc — though later emissions equipment, especially on
          US-market cars, quietly ate into the gains.
        </p>
        <p>
          Michelotti updated the design again for the 1971 MkIV, keeping the silhouette
          but modernizing the details and reworking the rear suspension for better
          cornering manners.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
